# Feature & Screen Pattern

## Folder layout

A feature is a folder under `lib/Screens/`. Each screen inside it has its own `Controller/` + `View/`.

```
Screens/Orders/
  OrdersScreen/        Controller/OrdersController.dart        View/OrdersScreen.dart
  OrderDetailsScreen/  Controller/OrderDetailsController.dart  View/OrderDetailsScreen.dart
  Widgets/             # widgets shared only inside this feature
```

Small features with 1–2 screens may flatten: `Screens/Profile/{Controller,View,Widgets}/`.
Purely visual screens with no state/logic (static info) may skip the Controller.

## Controller (plain Dart class, NOT a Cubit)

Holds all state + logic for one screen. Receives a `refresh` callback from the View.

```dart
class OrdersScreenController {
  OrdersScreenController(this.refresh, {this.loadOrders = OrdersApi.getAll});

  final void Function() refresh;
  final Future<KApiResponse> Function({required OrdersFilterModel filter}) loadOrders;

  final searchController = TextEditingController();
  List<OrderModel> list = [];
  bool loading = false;
  bool _disposed = false;

  void update() {
    if (!_disposed) refresh();
  }

  Future<void> onInit() => getData();

  Future<void> getData() async {
    loading = true;
    update();
    final response = await loadOrders(filter: OrdersFilterModel());
    if (_disposed) return;
    if (response.success) list = response.data as List<OrderModel>;
    loading = false;
    update();
  }

  void dispose() {
    _disposed = true;
    searchController.dispose();
  }
}
```

- Dependencies that hit the network or navigate (Api functions, `open...` routes, `AppCubit`) are
  constructor parameters with real defaults, so tests can replace them.
- Every `await` is followed by a disposed/mounted check before touching state.
- The Controller owns and disposes its `TextEditingController`s, `Timer`s, `StreamSubscription`s.
  `AnimationController`s need a `TickerProvider`, so they stay in the View.

## View (StatefulWidget)

```dart
class OrdersScreen extends StatefulWidget {
  const OrdersScreen({super.key});
  static const String routeName = '/OrdersScreen';
  @override
  State<OrdersScreen> createState() => _OrdersScreenState();
}

class _OrdersScreenState extends State<OrdersScreen> {
  late final OrdersScreenController _controller;

  @override
  void initState() {
    super.initState();
    _controller = OrdersScreenController(refresh);
    _controller.onInit();
  }

  void refresh() {
    if (mounted) setState(() {});
  }

  @override
  void dispose() {
    _controller.dispose();
    super.dispose();
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => /* read only from _controller / AppCubit */;
}
```

## Rules

- Every screen has `static const String routeName` (and `static const String path` if its path differs).
- Name the controller `<Screen>Controller`; if that collides with a Flutter class, use `<Screen>ScreenController`.
- Views contain no API calls and no business math; they read `_controller.*` and call controller methods.
- Controller mutates its fields then calls `update()`. Never call `setState` from the controller.
- Every screen handles loading, error (with retry), and empty states explicitly.
- Tab screens that must keep state use `AutomaticKeepAliveClientMixin`.
- Paginated lists → `LoadingListView` (optional module, `optional-modules.mdc`).
- Cross-screen results go back through callbacks (`onAdd`, `onUpdate`, `onDelete`) passed via the
  Routes `open...` function; app-wide changes go through `AppCubit`.
- User-facing strings come from `S.of(context)`; never hardcode UI text.
- `Components/<Name>/` follow the same Controller + View split when they have state.
